A Poem About Einstein

In Ulm, a patent clerk was born,
Whose thoughts would tear the fabric of the morn.
With paper, pen, and restless mind,
He left the old, slow world behind.

He saw the light, a constant race,
Through space and time, a bending face.
He said, "Let energy and mass
Be bound by c-squared, first to last."

He dreamed of atoms, small and strange,
Whose dance could shift the world's whole range.
He showed us that the shortest line
Is curved by gravity's design.

He taught us that the universe
Is stranger than our little verse—
That time can slow, that space can bend,
That matter and its fate must blend.

From E=mc² to the quantum's leap,
His equations, deep and steep,
